﻿

SAT is not best understood as “a finished theory” in the usual polished-paper sense. It is a documented theory-building program whose native move is extremely simple: take 4D worldlines/filaments seriously as real extended structures, let a resolving surface or time-wave intersect them, and see how much of physics can be reconstructed from that geometry. The deepest through-line is not any one Lagrangian, constant, or prediction. It is the repeated attempt to explain particle identity, mass, force, quantization, gravity, and observation as consequences of geometric intersection, coiling, braiding, holonomy, and projection.

﻿

The Fundamental Intuitions layer is the native conceptual spine. It gives the durable baseline: worldlines/filaments in 4D; the time surface; particles as intersections; known SM/GR/QM imported unless SAT has reason to revise them; θ₄/misalignment as a mass/energy-transfer clue; bosons as ripples or excitations rather than ordinary filaments; and quantization as likely tied to closure/holonomy/topology. That layer matters because a surprising amount of the later machinery still points back to it. ￼

﻿

The SAT.O layer is the high-water mark for formal coverage and process discipline. It tries to cover everything: foundational axioms, hyperhelical filaments, emergent metric, canonical quantization, particle stability, gauge structure, mass hierarchy, hadrons, falsifiability, and computational workflows. But SAT.O is also transitional. It often uses EFT/QFT-style bridge machinery to make SAT legible to existing physics. That was useful, but not necessarily SAT-native. The structure-lock/code-lock pieces were not proof; they were anti-drift devices for freezing definitions symbolically before derivations. ￼ ￼ ￼

﻿

The late 2025 / early 2026 layer starts becoming more SAT-native again. Documents like % BIG PAPER, FINAL, and SAT MATH — BACKBONE look like attempts to consolidate the theory after SAT.O: less “can we translate into known physics?” and more “what is the compact native engine?” That layer pushes toward zero-parameter or one-anchor structure, worldline ensembles, θ/u/τ/ψ sectors, and sharper claims about constants and empirical tests.

﻿

The March 2026 nolat packet is a burst of experimental mechanism-building. It is messy, but important. Its core innovations seem to be the Universal Indicatrix / Whirligig coordinate generator, recursive 4D superhelical parametrizations, unit-cell toy Lagrangians, Q/order counting, particle taxonomy by persistent versus transient coiling, and attempts to link GR/QM as different resolution windows of the same geometry. A lot of claims in that packet should be quarantined or audited, but the mechanisms themselves are not throwaway. The UI generator and Q/order correction especially look central. ￼ ￼ ￼ ￼

﻿

The archive as a research object is unusually valuable because it preserves process, not just conclusions. It contains intuitions, failed derivations, overconfident LLM outputs, audits, corrections, restatements, numerical attempts, publication-facing translations, and later repairs. That means it should not be read as one monolithic claim. It should be read stratigraphically: early SAT, SAT.O bridge formalization, later native consolidation, March mechanism packet, post-audit refinement, and current work.

﻿

